## Design decisions

**Provenance decides the provider, not the hostname.** Sniffing for
`*.databricks.com` breaks on custom DNS, private workspaces and proxies, and picks
the wrong credential silently when it guesses wrong. Instead: if
`ONTOBRICKS_LLM_BASE_URL` is set the caller wants an OpenAI-compatible provider;
otherwise the caller's triple is a workspace. No third `ONTOBRICKS_LLM_API_STYLE`
knob, because the answer is already knowable. A dataset row pins the awkward case
down: a *Databricks* host reached through the explicit external variables is
treated as OpenAI-style, deliberately.

**The 11 `run_agent(host, token, endpoint_name, …)` signatures do not change.**
The honest names would be `(base_url, api_key, model)`, and `call_serving_endpoint`
is now a misnomer. Renaming means 11 engine files plus 12 test modules that patch
the symbol by name — roughly 40 lines of pure churn mixed into a behaviour change,
which is the shape of diff that hides a real bug from a reviewer. The three
positional values already mean "endpoint base, credential, model name" in both
worlds. **Recorded as a follow-up**, to be done as a mechanical commit of its own,
not quietly dropped.

**`ONTOBRICKS_LLM_MODEL` is optional.** Without it, the caller's `endpoint_name`
becomes the model, which means the existing per-domain Domain Settings dropdown
keeps working — as a *model* picker instead of a serving-endpoint picker. That
preserved an existing capability rather than replacing it with a fixed value.

**`_UNSUPPORTED_PARAMS` re-keyed from `endpoint_name` to the resolved model.**
Parameter support is a property of the model. On an external provider the endpoint
name may be a stale Databricks value shared by several models, and a `temperature`
ban discovered for one would have silenced a parameter the other supports.

## Why the eval is contract-shaped

This is a transport change, so the meaningful property is behavioural
*equivalence*, not answer quality: pointed at the same model, an agent must produce
the output it produces today. A judge scoring answer quality cannot distinguish a
broken transport from a bad answer — a 500 and a hallucination both score zero.

So the five dimensions are `url_exact`, `auth_header_exact`, `payload_shape`,
`no_secret_leak` (all offline, weights summing to 0.90) and `live_smoke` (0.10).
The offline four reach the declared 0.90 aggregate deliberately, because CI has no
model to call.

**`live_smoke` did not run.** Every Databricks CLI profile on this machine has an
expired refresh token (`databricks auth login` needed, which is interactive) and no
external provider key is configured. It is scored **zero, not skipped**, so the
reported 0.9000 cannot be read as a verified end-to-end call against a real
provider. `.planning/agents/engine_base/PLAN.md` has the two commands that finish
it.

What *was* proven end-to-end: a real HTTP round-trip through the production
transport against a local OpenAI-compatible server, twice — once external with
every `DATABRICKS_*` variable deleted from the environment, once with the
Databricks preset. Both times 8/8 checks: URL shape, `Authorization`, `User-Agent`,
`model` present-or-absent per dialect, tool pass-through, temperature, response
extraction, usage accumulation. That proves the transport, not any provider's API.

## Gate hole found while doing this

`.github/workflows/eval-gate.yml` detected changed agents with
`grep -E '^src/agents/agent_[a-z_]+/'`. `src/agents/engine_base.py` does not match
that pattern, so `changed_agents` came back empty and **every gate job skipped**
via `if: needs.detect.outputs.changed_agents != ''`. The one file shared by all 11
agents — the highest-leverage file in the tree — was invisible to the gate that
exists to protect them.

Rather than quietly benefit from the hole, the detector now maps shared-infra paths
(`engine_base.py`, `llm_utils.py`, `tracing.py`, `serialization.py`) to a sentinel
subject `engine_base`, which carries its own SPEC and dataset. Simulated against
this change's own file list, under bash: subject `engine_base`, SPEC found, dataset
24 rows, eval-dimensions table parses.

(The simulation initially reported everything missing — because zsh does not
word-split unquoted variables and GitHub Actions runs bash. The gate logic was
fine; my harness was wrong.)

